<title>USA: U.S. Dec producer prices rose 0.5 pct.</title>
<headline>U.S. Dec producer prices rose 0.5 pct.</headline>
<dateline>WASHINGTON 1997-01-09</dateline>
<text>
<p>U.S. Labor Department monthly producer price index (1982 equals 100):</p>
<p>    Percent Changes:   Seasonally Adj.  Unadjusted</p>
<p> -				 Dec    Nov   Dec96/95</p>
<p> Finished Goods		0.5    0.4     2.8</p>
<p> -Less Food, Energy	  0.1    0.1     0.6</p>
<p> -Consumer Foods	    -0.1   -0.1     3.4</p>
<p> -Energy Goods		 3.1    2.3    12.0</p>
<p> Finished Goods Index-X  132.7  132.5     N/A</p>
<p>X-Data not seasonally adjusted. N/A-Not Available.  </p>
<p>    Percent Changes:   Seasonally Adj.  Unadjusted</p>
<p> Finished Goods		Dec    Nov   Dec96/95</p>
<p> -Gasoline		     5.2    2.8    28.8</p>
<p> -Heating Oil		  4.9   -0.4    26.3</p>
<p> -Tobacco Products	   0.3   -0.1     2.7</p>
<p> -Passenger Cars	     0.3    0.5    -0.7</p>
<p> -Capital Equipment	  0.1    0.3     0.5</p>
<p> Intermediate Goods	  0.4    0.2     0.8</p>
<p> -Less Food, Energy	  0.1    0.1    -0.9</p>
<p> -Manufact Materials	 0.2   Unch    -1.2</p>
<p> -Construction Materials  Unch    0.4     1.9</p>
<p>    Percent Changes:   Seasonally Adj.  Unadjusted</p>
<p> Intermediate Goods	  Dec    Nov   Dec96/95</p>
<p> -Intermed.Energy Goods    2.5    1.2    10.8</p>
<p> Crude Goods		   4.2    1.8    12.2</p>
<p> -Less Food, Energy-X	0.2   -0.3    -5.6</p>
<p> -Food/Feedstuffs	   -4.3   -1.9    -0.9</p>
<p> -Nonfood Materials	 10.5    4.7    22.4</p>
<p> -Energy Materials-Y	16.5    7.7    43.2</p>
<p> -Petroleum		    3.9   -4.6    36.2</p>
<p>X-Excludes crude petroleum. Y-Includes crude petroleum. Note - Tobacco products and petroleum are not seasonally adjusted.  </p>
<p>Wall Street economists had forecast December producer prices to rise 0.3 percent. Excluding food and energy prices, they expected a 0.1 percent rise.  </p>
<p>The department said the rise in the December PPI was the largest since a 0.6 percent gain in December 1995. Also, energy prices in December posted their largest increase since 3.8 percent in December 1995, while the price of crude goods rose at its fastest rate since 4.5 percent in April 1996.</p>
<p>For 1996 overall, the PPI was up 2.8 percent after an increase of 2.3 percent in 1995. Excluding food and energy, the PPI rose 0.6 percent in 1996 versus 2.6 percent the previous year. The rise excluding food and energy was the smallest since a 0.4 percent increase in 1993.</p>
